# Employee Requirements Tracker — environment configuration
#
# Copy to `.env`, then UNCOMMENT AND FILL only the lines you actually need.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# ── Two things about this file that are not the usual convention ──────────────
#
# 1. Nothing loads it. The application reads System.getenv directly and there is
#    no dotenv library on the classpath, so `.env` is inert until you load it
#    yourself:
#
#        set -a; source .env; set +a; ./kotlin run
#
#    or point your IDE run configuration at it.
#
# 2. Every key is commented out on purpose. An exported-but-empty variable is
#    not the same as an unset one — `System.getenv` returns "" rather than null,
#    and most defaults below are reached by a null check. `DATABASE_URL=` would
#    fail startup in Hikari, and `JWT_ISSUER=` would silently break token
#    verification. Leaving a key commented is what preserves its default.
#
# Everything here has a working default EXCEPT when APP_ENV is set to something
# other than `dev` — see JWT_SECRET and TOKEN_PEPPER.


# ── Runtime ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which deployment this is. Unset or `dev` means development: /openapi, /swagger
# and /metrics are open, and JWT_SECRET and TOKEN_PEPPER may be omitted. Any
# other value puts all three behind HR authentication and makes both secrets
# mandatory.
#
# SET THIS IN EVERY NON-LOCAL DEPLOYMENT. Four controls hang off it, and
# forgetting it does not fail — it silently selects the permissive configuration.
# Expected: dev | staging | production
# APP_ENV=

# TCP port the server binds. Default 8080.
# PORT=

# Use case tracing (ERT-195). Unset means off, and off means a no-op is bound --
# nothing is measured and nothing is logged. Set it to `true` while debugging to
# get one DEBUG line per use case on the `usecase` logger, carrying the use case
# name, the outcome (`ok` or the AppError code) and the elapsed milliseconds,
# tagged with the request id that `%X{requestId}` prints on every other line.
#
# It never logs arguments, so it carries no PIN, token, filename or email. Only
# `true` and `false` are understood; anything else warns and leaves tracing off.
# Enabling it outside APP_ENV=dev warns but is allowed.
#
# Volume is a separate knob: `<logger name="usecase">` in resources/logback.xml.
# Expected: true | false
# TRACE_USECASES=


# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# JDBC URL. Unset means an in-memory H2 in PostgreSQL mode, so a fresh checkout
# and the test suite need no external service. PostgreSQL is the production
# target; Flyway migrates the schema on startup.
# Expected: jdbc:postgresql://<host>:5432/<database>
# DATABASE_URL=

# Role owning the schema. Default `sa`, which suits the H2 fallback.
# DATABASE_USER=

# Default empty, which suits the H2 fallback.
# DATABASE_PASSWORD=

# HikariCP maximum pool size, an integer. Default 10.
# DATABASE_MAX_POOL_SIZE=


# ── HR authentication ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# PRD §14 Q4 was answered on 2026-09-16: a handful of HR staff, local accounts,
# two roles, bcrypt, NO SSO. The JWT mechanism survives; the placeholder framing
# does not. ERT-190 replaces the per-run random key with tokens issued against a
# `users` row — see the HR accounts block further down for JWT_TTL_MINUTES and
# the bootstrap account.

# REQUIRED outside dev: startup refuses rather than boot on a default key. In dev
# an ephemeral key is generated per run, so tokens do not survive a restart.
# Expected: a high-entropy random string, 32+ characters, unique per environment.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# JWT_SECRET=

# Default http://localhost:8080/
# JWT_ISSUER=

# Default employee-requirements-tracker
# JWT_AUDIENCE=

# Default "Employee Requirements Tracker"
# JWT_REALM=


# ── Portal link credential ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Server-side pepper keying the HMAC-SHA-256 digest of upload-link and portal
# session tokens. Keying is what stops a leaked token_hash column from being
# recomputed against a candidate list; determinism is what lets a presented token
# be resolved by its digest at all.
#
# REQUIRED outside dev: startup refuses without it. In dev an ephemeral pepper is
# generated, so links issued before a restart stop resolving.
#
# MUST BE AT LEAST 32 CHARACTERS, in dev too. A short pepper satisfies "is it
# configured" while adding no work to an offline attempt.
#
# ROTATING THIS INVALIDATES EVERY LIVE LINK AND SESSION. There is no credential
# re-issue flow, so rotation currently means re-inviting every in-flight hire by
# hand. Treat it as permanent for the life of an environment -- until ERT-1130
# adds a two-pepper verification window and a bulk re-issue.
#
# Expected: a high-entropy random string, distinct from JWT_SECRET.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# TOKEN_PEPPER=


# ── CORS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Comma-separated hosts allowed to call the API cross-origin. https only, and
# hosts carry no scheme or trailing slash. Unset means no cross-origin host is
# permitted, which is the correct default for a same-origin deployment.
# Expected: hr.example.com,portal.example.com
# CORS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=


# ── HR accounts (ERT-190) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRD 14 Q4 was answered 2026-09-16: a handful of HR staff, LOCAL accounts held
# in this system, two roles, bcrypt, no SSO. These land with ERT-190; until it
# ships, the JWT scheme signs with a per-run random key in dev and nothing can
# mint a token the application accepts.

# How long an issued HR token stays valid, in minutes. Default 60.
#
# This is the REVOCATION WINDOW and it is a deliberate trade. The verifier checks
# claims only and does not resolve the subject against `users` on every request,
# so deactivating an account takes effect within this many minutes rather than
# immediately. The immediate control for an account disabled for cause is
# revoking what the person can reach, not the token.
# JWT_TTL_MINUTES=

# The first HR_ADMIN, created ONLY when the `users` table is empty, with
# password_change_required set so it must be changed at first sign-in.
#
# REQUIRED outside dev when the table is empty: startup refuses rather than
# coming up with no way in, or with a known way in. Same shape as JWT_SECRET and
# TOKEN_PEPPER, for the same reason.
# HR_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=
# HR_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=


# ── Portal (ERT-440) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Origin the invitation link points at, with no trailing slash.
#
# REQUIRED outside dev. The invitation body contains a link, and without this
# there is no origin to build one from -- ERT-440 cannot render a message at all.
# Expected: https://portal.example.com
# PORTAL_BASE_URL=


# ── Document storage (ERT-710) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRD 14 Q20 was answered 2026-09-16: GCP Cloud Storage in production, with V4
# signed URLs. The filesystem adapter below serves dev and tests behind the same
# port, and mints its own expiring MAC'd URL so that ERT-810 does not change when
# the provider lands.

# Directory the filesystem adapter writes under. MUST be outside the repository.
# Keys are opaque by design -- {employeeId}/{requirementId}/v{n}/{random}, with no
# filename component, because a filename in a key leaks content through logs and
# URLs exactly as the document does.
# Expected: /var/lib/ert/documents
# STORAGE_ROOT=

# Comma-separated MIME allowlist, enforced server-side on SNIFFED CONTENT rather
# than on the declared header, which is attacker-controlled (PRD 12, Q21).
#
# Default: image/jpeg,image/png,image/heic,image/heif,application/pdf
#
# That is the 8.4 preview set, and the property it buys is that every accepted
# file is one HR can look at in the browser. A format HR must download to read is
# a format that gets reviewed less carefully, and careful review is the control
# 8.5 depends on. Adding a type should be a decision, not a permissive default.
# UPLOAD_MIME_ALLOWLIST=


# ── Mail (ERT-1010) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRD 14 Q12 was answered 2026-09-16: an SMTP relay on internal mail. Until
# ERT-1010 ships, MAIL_ENABLED stays false and ERT-440's outbox holds everything.

# Master switch. Unset or false binds the outbox adapter and TRANSMITS NOTHING.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS OFF ON PURPOSE. A staging box pointed at the real relay with
# production-shaped seed data will email real candidates, and there is no way to
# un-send an invitation carrying a live link.
# Expected: true | false
# MAIL_ENABLED=

# When set, every recipient is rewritten to this address and the subject names
# the intended one. The safe way to test a real relay.
# MAIL_REDIRECT_TO=

# REQUIRED when MAIL_ENABLED is true: startup fails without SMTP_HOST and
# MAIL_FROM.
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=
# SMTP_USERNAME=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_STARTTLS=

# The sending identity. Its domain needs SPF, DKIM and DMARC alignment, which is
# IT's half of Q12 and sits on the Phase 1 exit checklist: an invitation that
# lands in spam is indistinguishable from one never sent.
# MAIL_FROM=
# MAIL_FROM_NAME=
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# Outbox poller. Defaults: 30 seconds, 5 attempts.
# Backoff is 1m, 5m, 15m, 1h, 4h; a permanent SMTP 5xx fails terminally on the
# first attempt rather than retrying an unknown recipient five times.
# OUTBOX_POLL_SECONDS=
# OUTBOX_MAX_ATTEMPTS=
